<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Żagań</h1><p class="st-meta">1274 CE – 1786 CE (approx.) · capital Żagań</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Silesian duchy that passed from the Piasts to the Wettins, Wallenstein and the Lobkowicz, and where Johannes Kepler worked for a time.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a>, in north-western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a>, became the seat of a separate duchy in the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> lands after 1274. It was ruled by branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Głogów Piasts</a>, who accepted Bohemian overlordship in the 1320s and 1330s, and Żagań grew as a market town with a large Augustinian abbey.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> dukes ruled until the turbulent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-ii-the-mad">Jan II the Mad</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins of Saxony</a> in 1472. It later returned to the Bohemian crown and in 1628 was granted to the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>, who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> there. From 1646 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lobkowicz">Lobkowicz family</a> held it, building a baroque palace.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in 1786 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lobkowicz">Lobkowicz</a> sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-von-biron">Peter von Biron</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorothea-von-biron">Dorothea</a> later held it as Duchess of Sagan. The town has been Polish since 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-zagan.jpg" alt="Duchy of Żagań" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Zuber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silesia_1278-1281.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1274 CE – 1786 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Żagań</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan II the Mad, Albrecht von Wallenstein, Wenzel Eusebius von Lobkowicz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Sagan, Duchy of Zagan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=duchy-of-zagan" aria-label="Duchy of Żagań on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5026 392 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Żagań lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5346 648L5363 646M5346 648L5330 692M5346 648L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5363 646h0M5330 692h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5346 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5346 648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Żagań. <a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=duchy-of-zagan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
